// Node.js pseudo-code for validator
const registry = getRegistryFromDynamoDB();
function validateEvent(evt){
  const v = evt.version;
  const rules = registry[v];
  if(!rules) return {ok:false, reason:'unsupported version'};
  // basic field checks per rules
  return {ok:true};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test this setup using LocalStack or mocks?
- How would you evolve the schema while preserving backwards compatibility?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes practical lineage, idempotence, and observability, not just throughput.

## Key Concepts
- Data lineage across KDS, Fargate, and S3
- Immutable raw storage and audit tracking
- Idempotent writes and failure handling
- Observability via CloudWatch and Athena

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal conditional put to DynamoDB to upsert audit records
const { DynamoDBClient, PutItemCommand } = require('@aws-sdk/client-dynamodb');
const client = new DynamoDBClient({region: 'us-east-1'});
const cmd = new PutItemCommand({
  TableName: 'TelemetryAudit',
  Item: {
    event_id: { S: id },
    shard_id: { S: shard },
    ingest_ts: { N: String(ingest) },
    process_ts: { N: String(now) },
    status: { S: 'OK' },
    transformations_hash: { S: hash }
  },
  ConditionExpression: 'attribute_not_exists(event_id)'
});
await client.send(cmd);
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you test end-to-end lineage in a sandbox? 
- How would you handle schema evolution while preserving lineage?

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests practical understanding of end-to-end streaming pipelines, data quality, and data privacy basics in a beginner-friendly AWS DVA context. It emphasizes real-world concerns like in-flight redaction, idempotent writes, and observability.

## Key Concepts

- AWS DVA: Kinesis, Lambda preprocessing, DynamoDB dedupe, S3 Parquet storage, DLQ, X-Ray
- Data privacy: redacting PII in transit
- Data quality: required fields, schema validation, backfill

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo-code: Lambda processing Kinesis records
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  for (const rec of event.Records) {
    const payload = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(rec.kinesis.data, 'base64').toString('utf-8'));
    // validate
    if (!payload.deviceId || typeof payload.ts !== 'number' || typeof payload.metric !== 'number') {
      // send to DLQ
      continue;
    }
    // redact
    if (payload.email) payload.email = '[REDACTED]';
    if (payload.phone) payload.phone = '[REDACTED]';
    // dedupe check via DynamoDB (deviceId#ts)
    // write to S3 Parquet
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this end-to-end with LocalStack and simulate 2k events/min?
- What changes would you make to scale the deduplication and backfill strategy when ts is out of order?

## Why This Is Asked
This interview question explores cross-region, multi-tenant data routing with dynamic policy-driven enrichment, end-to-end observability, and integration of AWS data primitives beyond basic pipelines.

## Key Concepts
- Cross-region routing with tenant isolation
- OPA-driven enrichment at ingestion with hot policy refresh via changelog stream
- Idempotent writes to S3 Parquet and DynamoDB
- AWS Glue catalog integration with Athena for analytics
- OpenTelemetry end-to-end tracing
- Per-tenant DLQ handling and retry strategy

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: ingestion Lambda
async function processRecord(record, ctx) {
  const tenantId = extractTenantId(record);
  const region = extractRegion(record);
  const enriched = await enrichWithOPARules(record, tenantId);
  await writeToS3Parquet(enriched, tenantId, region);
  await writeToDynamoDB(enriched, tenantId);
}
```

Why This Is Asked
This question adds an enrichment and hourly aggregation angle not covered by prior items, emphasizing idempotency, per-message dedup via DynamoDB, and end-to-end tracing.

Key Concepts
- Kinesis Data Streams and Lambda integration
- DynamoDB-based deduplication using messageId
- DynamoDB for device metadata enrichment
- Parquet storage in S3 with hourly partitioning
- AWS X-Ray tracing across Lambda steps
- LocalStack test planning for end-to-end validation

Code Example
```javascript
// enrich.js
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  const meta = await getDeviceMeta(event.deviceId);
  return { ...event, metadata: meta };
};
```

Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle bursty traffic and shard hot spots in Kinesis?
- How would you validate end-to-end exactly-once semantics given Lambda retries?

## Why This Is Asked
Privacy and compliance demand masking at ingestion, not post-hoc. The candidate should design an isolation boundary for unmasked data, ensure per-tenant access controls, and plan end-to-end lifecycle.

## Key Concepts
- Ingestion-time PII masking with tenant isolation
- Per-tenant KMS keys and IAM-based access controls
- Dual data stores: masked data in a shared lake and unmasked in a restricted vault
- Glue catalog and Athena for querying masked data; DynamoDB for audit trails
- Data lifecycle and testing strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
function maskPII(record, tenantKey) {
  // pseudo-implementation
  record.phone = redact(record.phone);
  record.name = redact(record.name);
  return record;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate masking correctness at scale?
- What are the latency and cost trade-offs of this approach?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical ingestion patterns, per-record validation, dedupe, and multi-destination storage, plus observability.

## Key Concepts

- Kinesis Data Streams, Lambda consumption
- DynamoDB for dedupe and latest state
- S3 Parquet storage with partitioning by date and deviceType
- DLQ via SQS for invalid records
- Glue Schema Registry optional validation
- AWS X-Ray tracing and LocalStack testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Lambda handler sketch: validate, upsert, write
exports.handler = async (event) => {
  for (const record of event.Records) {
    const payload = JSON.parse(Buffer.from(record.kinesis.data, 'base64').toString());
    // validate against schema (pseudo)
    const valid = validate(payload);
    if (!valid) {
      await dlqSend(payload);
      continue;
    }
    await updateLatest(payload);
    await writeParquet(payload);
  }
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle late events and out-of-order ts?  
- How would you scale for traffic bursts and optimize cost?  
- How would you test end-to-end with LocalStack and mock services?

## Why This Is Asked
This question probes end-to-end streaming design with multi-tenant isolation, regional processing, dynamic schema evolution, and observability. It requires concrete components (KDS, Glue Schema Registry, S3 Parquet, DynamoDB dedupe, DLQ) and addresses late data, SLA, and cost.

## Key Concepts
- Multi-tenant isolation and data governance
- Exactly-once processing and idempotent writes
- Dynamic schema validation and evolution
- Late data handling and backfill window
- Observability with OpenTelemetry and tracing, cost controls

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-idempotent writer
exports.handler = async (evt) => {
  const rec = JSON.parse(evt.body)
  const key = `${rec.tenantId}-${rec.eventId}`
  const exists = await docClient.get({TableName:'Dedup', Key:{id:key}}).promise()
  if (exists.Item) return {status: 200}
  await docClient.put({TableName:'Dedup', Item:{id:key, ts: Date.now()}}).promise()
  // write to S3 Parquet (omitted)
  return {status: 200}
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate schema changes without breaking existing data and roll out in multiple regions?
- What failure modes would you test and how would you monitor them end-to-end?
